  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nRain showers and weak thunderstorms are moving into the forecast \narea from the southwest this morning. Expectations over the next \ncouple of hours is that coverage of these showers and storms will \ncontinue to expand, covering most if not all of the forecast area by \nsunrise. These showers and storms are fueled by a 40-50 knot low-\nlevel jet moving across the region this morning, strengthening to 55 \nknots as it progresses eastward after sunrise. In addition to the \nstorm development, this low-level jet is expected to bring strong \nsoutherly gusts up to 40 miles per hour to much of northeastern \nSouth Dakota and western Minnesota this morning. Still anticipating \nthe highest rainfall totals over eastern South Dakota, and NBM \nmedian values through tonight sit around 1\"-1.25\" over Codington \nCounty and the surrounding counties. As mentioned in previous \ndiscussions, there is an outside chance of flooding potential given \nthe abnormally high PWAT values advected into the region (around \n1.5\"-1.75\", well above the 90th percentile for mid-September), full \nsaturation through the profile, and a warm cloud layer depth of \nroughly 11-12k feet. However, antecedent conditions are not very \nfavorable for flooding, and much of eastern South Dakota in \nparticular is under D0-D1 drought. Storm motion is also expected to \nbe fairly quick, although with the low-level jet in play the \ndevelopment of training storms cannot be ruled out. Perhaps the \nbiggest limiting factor though is the lack of CAPE. Generally \nexpecting less than 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE, although it should be noted \nthat values seem to be increasing over the past couple of forecast \ncycles. Still, not expecting much in the way of strong thunderstorms \nor severe weather with this development, but will continue to keep a \nclose eye through the morning hours. \n\nAfter the passage of the cold front, winds will turn northwesterly \nand increase. The strongest winds, gusting upwards of 35 miles per \nhour, are expected along and west of the Missouri River, \nparticularly over Corson and Dewey Counties. NBM probability of \nreaching 45 mile per hour gusts in those two counties sits in the 40-\n60% range from around noon Monday through midnight Monday night. \nElsewhere, both NBM and HREF probabilities of reaching wind advisory \ncriteria struggle to reach more than 20-30%. However the combination \nof the strong cold air advection coming in behind, a shallow diurnal \ninversion at the surface, and the tendency for the NBM in particular \nto verify near the 90th percentile under this type of setup, there \nis some slightly increased confidence given to needing an Advisory \nover Campbell/Walworth/McPherson/Edmunds Counties. Therefore, have \nexpanded the coverage on the previous advisory, running the new \nadditions from 00Z-09Z Tuesday. The potential failure point here \nwould be if the inversion holds, but given the expected temperature \ndrops at 850mb (nearly 10 degrees Celsius in 6 hours), the profile \nwill likely than not be able to maintain some degree of mixing to \nthe surface. \n\nIn addition to Advisory level criteria, winds during the afternoon \nwill be the driver behind some elevated fire weather concerns. A \nbrief warmup in temperatures this afternoon out ahead of the front \nover central and north central South Dakota will push afternoon \nhumidity down below 40 percent over the westernmost column of \ncounties in the Aberdeen CWA. The combination of the wind and low \nhumidity, in addition to fuel status considerations, leads to the \nGrassland Fire Danger Index reaching Very High in that area. \nCounties just to the east of the previously mentioned area will \nreach High Grassland Fire Danger as well.\n\nBy mid-week, the upper-level setup will transition to near-zonal \nflow and a positively tilted trough over the western CONUS. This \nsetup will allow for additional shortwaves into the region, leading \nto more chances for rain later in the week. Specifically, Wednesday \nand Friday both sport chances for rain at this time. Model soundings \nfor both of those days do not look particularly favorable for any \nsevere weather potential, although perhaps a touch to early to say \nfor certain on Friday in particular. Also expecting a gradual \nwarming trend with this setup, with median high temperatures \nreaching near-normal (mid-70s) over the weekend.",
